WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.880
We'll start with code that a
coding agent has already written.

00:00:04.220 --> 00:00:06.200
I'll review it locally in VS Code.

00:00:06.460 --> 00:00:09.880
Then open a pull request and walk
through the summary and findings.

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:13.880
We'll investigate a security
issue and a discount bug,

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:17.560
ask questions in the review, and
teach CodeRabbit a business rule.

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:21.580
Then we'll follow an AI generated
fix through the code and tests,

00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:23.960
and look at the separate
documentation change.

00:00:25.547 --> 00:00:28.207
In my Mac terminal, I'm
in the projects folder.

00:00:28.747 --> 00:00:30.947
I clone the repository
from GitHub and select

00:00:30.947 --> 00:00:32.607
the branch containing the implementation.

00:00:33.207 --> 00:00:35.167
Then I launch VS Code from this folder.

00:00:35.627 --> 00:00:38.387
This is my first look at the
code the agent generated.

00:00:38.987 --> 00:00:42.467
I open the CodeRabbit extension so
I can review the changes locally.

00:00:43.207 --> 00:00:45.627
The agent's work is already
committed to this branch.

00:00:45.987 --> 00:00:49.527
I choose main as the base and
select review committed changes.

00:00:49.947 --> 00:00:51.847
That compares this branch with main.

00:00:52.327 --> 00:00:55.507
Even with no pending edits, there
are committed changes to review.

00:00:56.147 --> 00:00:57.547
I start the review.

00:00:57.947 --> 00:01:00.727
When the results are ready,
I open the security finding.

00:01:01.407 --> 00:01:04.727
CodeRabbit has identified a SQL
injection issue in invoice search.

00:01:05.427 --> 00:01:08.627
Opening the finding takes me to the
affected code and the explanation.

00:01:09.407 --> 00:01:13.087
Search text is being inserted directly
into the database query which could

00:01:13.087 --> 00:01:18.027
let someone change the query and
retrieve another customer's invoice.

00:01:20.107 --> 00:01:22.647
The agent generated tests
and those tests passed.

00:01:22.867 --> 00:01:26.787
This review still found a security
problem before I opened the pull request.

00:01:27.827 --> 00:01:29.787
Next I open the discount finding.

00:01:30.427 --> 00:01:34.187
The discount should apply at 10 items
but the code checks for more than 10.

00:01:34.507 --> 00:01:37.347
An order of exactly 10
items pays full price.

00:01:37.887 --> 00:01:41.647
The suggested fix changes greater
than to greater than or equal.

00:01:42.167 --> 00:01:45.467
That includes the minimum quantity
as the business rule requires.

00:01:46.167 --> 00:01:48.547
The findings also give me ways to respond.

00:01:49.627 --> 00:01:52.847
I can apply a suggested fix
for a simple change or use fix

00:01:52.847 --> 00:01:56.527
with AI to pass the finding and
its context to my coding agent.

00:01:57.207 --> 00:01:59.987
Fix All lets me hand over
the review findings together.

00:02:01.727 --> 00:02:03.807
After a fix, I would inspect the diff,

00:02:03.987 --> 00:02:06.407
run the relevant tests and
review the updated code.

00:02:06.847 --> 00:02:10.967
I can do that work here on my branch
before asking the team to review it.

00:02:11.467 --> 00:02:14.047
For this walkthrough, I'll
carry the example into a

00:02:14.047 --> 00:02:17.027
pull request so we can see
the team's review experience.

00:02:18.207 --> 00:02:21.307
I am now on GitHub opening
a pull request against main.

00:02:21.787 --> 00:02:24.827
The description links the
requirements for promotional pricing,

00:02:25.087 --> 00:02:26.847
checkout and invoice search and export.

00:02:27.687 --> 00:02:30.807
Once the pull request is created,
CodeRabbit starts its review.

00:02:31.347 --> 00:02:32.927
I open the walkthrough first.

00:02:33.027 --> 00:02:36.367
It summarizes the implementation
and the areas it touches so

00:02:36.367 --> 00:02:39.247
I can understand the scope before
reading individual findings.

00:02:40.077 --> 00:02:42.747
Next, I scroll to the pre -merged checks.

00:02:43.787 --> 00:02:46.087
Security gate has failed
because this review

00:02:46.087 --> 00:02:49.007
found major and critical security issues.

00:02:49.527 --> 00:02:52.787
The linked issues check also
flags acceptance criteria the

00:02:52.787 --> 00:02:55.727
implementation has missed,
including the discount threshold.

00:02:56.207 --> 00:02:59.487
I follow the security finding
to the invoice search query.

00:03:00.107 --> 00:03:02.047
It's the same problem we saw locally.

00:03:02.227 --> 00:03:04.907
Search text goes straight
into the SQL command.

00:03:05.287 --> 00:03:08.667
The finding identifies the affected
line and explains how crafted

00:03:08.667 --> 00:03:11.787
input could expose invoices
outside the customer's account.

00:03:14.347 --> 00:03:17.467
The proposed correction uses
parameters so the database

00:03:17.467 --> 00:03:20.807
receives the search text as a
value separate from the command.

00:03:21.247 --> 00:03:22.927
The discount finding is here too.

00:03:23.547 --> 00:03:27.547
Exactly 10 items should qualify and
the current comparison excludes them.

00:03:27.987 --> 00:03:31.727
I can follow the explanation directly
to the condition that needs to change.

00:03:32.247 --> 00:03:35.147
There's also a compatibility
issue across repositories.

00:03:35.887 --> 00:03:37.767
This change returns a field called total

00:03:37.767 --> 00:03:41.207
while a linked consumer expects totalCents.

00:03:42.127 --> 00:03:45.207
CodeRabbit points to the consumer
code that depends on that contract.

00:03:45.627 --> 00:03:48.427
That gives me a concrete
reason to investigate even

00:03:48.427 --> 00:03:51.187
if this application's own
screen appears to work.

00:03:52.407 --> 00:03:54.447
Finally, I check the review configuration.

00:03:55.447 --> 00:03:56.547
Request changes is enabled.

00:03:57.227 --> 00:04:00.567
Whether those requests prevent a
merge depends on the repository's

00:04:00.567 --> 00:04:04.227
branch rules, which we need to
check alongside the review results.

00:04:06.427 --> 00:04:09.647
I open the discount findings
conversation to add a business rule.

00:04:10.127 --> 00:04:12.787
Exactly 10 paid items should
qualify for the discount.

00:04:13.207 --> 00:04:15.907
Free promotional items should
not count toward that minimum.

00:04:16.947 --> 00:04:19.907
I explain the distinction and
ask CodeRabbit to remember it.

00:04:20.227 --> 00:04:23.767
At this point, I'm asking you to understand
the rule before changing the code.

00:04:24.287 --> 00:04:27.007
The reply confirms that
eligibility depends on

00:04:27.007 --> 00:04:30.067
paid items and that exactly
10 paid items qualify.

00:04:30.627 --> 00:04:34.887
It also confirms that free promotional
items do not count toward the minimum.

00:04:35.507 --> 00:04:37.187
The rule is captured as a learning.

00:04:37.787 --> 00:04:40.287
That gives later eligible
reviews additional context

00:04:40.287 --> 00:04:42.907
about how this team expects
the feature to behave.

00:04:43.767 --> 00:04:45.987
Now I return to the SQL injection finding.

00:04:46.407 --> 00:04:48.607
I ask CodeRabbit to explain
the problem in plain

00:04:48.607 --> 00:04:51.187
language and give examples of
this type of vulnerability.

00:04:51.807 --> 00:04:55.147
The answer uses an ordinary
search to explain the risk.

00:04:55.327 --> 00:04:57.567
A word like laptop should
be treated as text.

00:04:58.107 --> 00:05:00.707
In this query, specially
crafted text can change the

00:05:00.707 --> 00:05:03.707
database command and expose
another customer's invoices.

00:05:04.527 --> 00:05:07.767
It also explains why a character
limit does not fix the problem

00:05:08.307 --> 00:05:12.107
and why parameters keep search
values separate from the command.

00:05:12.727 --> 00:05:17.707
The reply includes examples of the same
vulnerability class in other systems.

00:05:18.387 --> 00:05:21.367
I can ask a follow up
here with the relevant

00:05:21.367 --> 00:05:24.307
code and the original
finding still in view.

00:05:24.707 --> 00:05:28.627
That helps me understand what the fix
needs to protect before I request a change.

00:05:29.607 --> 00:05:33.567
I return to the discount finding and
ask AI to fix it from the review thread.

00:05:34.027 --> 00:05:36.367
The request includes the affected code and

00:05:36.367 --> 00:05:38.087
the explanation of the boundary problem.

00:05:38.847 --> 00:05:41.467
When the generated change
is ready, I open the diff.

00:05:42.327 --> 00:05:44.247
Greater than becomes greater than or equal,

00:05:44.347 --> 00:05:47.227
so an order of exactly 10
items now meets the minimum.

00:05:47.587 --> 00:05:51.527
The regression test for that boundary
becomes a normal passing assertion.

00:05:52.087 --> 00:05:56.227
The focus checks cover quantities
below, at and above the minimum.

00:05:56.747 --> 00:06:00.047
They also confirm that an expired
promotion remains ineligible.

00:06:00.947 --> 00:06:02.847
After validating the generated commit,

00:06:02.987 --> 00:06:05.887
we apply that same commit
to the pull request branch.

00:06:06.487 --> 00:06:08.547
This patch addresses the boundary bug.

00:06:08.747 --> 00:06:11.147
The other findings still
need their own attention.

00:06:12.607 --> 00:06:14.187
There's another action I want to show.

00:06:14.747 --> 00:06:19.507
Under finishing touches, I select generate
docstrings and choose create new PR.

00:06:20.207 --> 00:06:22.967
That prepares a separate
documentation change for review.

00:06:23.507 --> 00:06:26.007
I open the resulting pull
request and inspect the diff.

00:06:26.407 --> 00:06:29.287
The new comments explain
what the functions expect,

00:06:29.507 --> 00:06:31.827
what they return, and
how they handle errors.

00:06:32.767 --> 00:06:37.227
For this example, this docstring
describes the catalog subtotal

00:06:37.227 --> 00:06:40.247
in cents and what happens when
a product cannot be found.

00:06:40.807 --> 00:06:45.307
Beside it, the request parser documents
the product ID and quantity requirements.

00:06:45.857 --> 00:06:49.427
We check that removing the
comments leaves the code unchanged.

00:06:50.067 --> 00:06:53.687
I still read the descriptions against
implementation before accepting them.

00:06:54.267 --> 00:06:57.327
We've reviewed the code locally,
examined the pull request,

00:06:57.507 --> 00:07:00.587
clarified the findings, and
inspected an AI generated fix.

00:07:01.147 --> 00:07:03.387
The resulting changes are
ready for the team's review.
